<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-comparison-with-known-quantities" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Comparison with Known Quantities
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      There are many more series convergence tests which follow from the <xref ref="theorem-direct-comparison-test" text="title"/>. Direct comparisons with series which are well known to converge or diverge can be used to provide additional criteria sufficient to conclude convergence or divergence. The following two examples, the <term> ratio test </term> and the <term> root test </term>, follow in part from direct comparison with geometric series.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-ratio-test">
    <title>
      Ratio test
    </title>
    
    <creator>
      Jean Le Rond d'Alembert
    </creator>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of non-zero real numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \R \setminus \set{ 0 } </m>.
        
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              If
              
              <md>
                \limsup_{ n \to \infty }{ \abs{ \frac{ a_{ n + 1 } }{ a_{ n } } } } \lt 1
              </md>,
              
              then the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ a_{ n } } </m> converges absolutely.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              If
              
              <md>
                \liminf_{ n \to \infty }{ \abs{ \frac{ a_{ n + 1 } }{ a_{ n } } } } \gt 1
              </md>,
              
              then the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ a_{ n } } </m> diverges.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Let <m> L = \limsup_{ n \to \infty }{ \abs{ \frac{ a_{ n + 1 } }{ a_{ n } } } } \lt 1 </m>, and consider the positive real number
            
            <md>
              r = \frac{ L + 1 }{ 2 } = L + \frac{ 1 - L }{ 2 } \lt 1
            </md>
            
            Then there is a natural number <m> m \in \N </m> so that <m> \abs{ \frac{ a_{ n + 1 } }{ a_{ n } } } \leq r </m> for all indices <m> n \geq m </m>. In particular, we see that
            
            <md>
              0 \leq \abs{ a_{ n } } \leq \of{ \frac{ \abs{ a_{ m } } }{ r^{ m } } } r^{ n }
            </md>
            
            for all indices <m> n \geq m </m>. The <xref ref="theorem-direct-comparison-test" text="title"/> now implies that the series
            
            <md>
              \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \abs{ a_{ n } } }
            </md>
            
            converges, and so the series
            
            <md>
              \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ a_{ n } }
            </md>
            
            converges absolutely by <xref ref="corollary-absolute-convergence-implies-convergence"/>.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            Let <m> L = \liminf_{ n \to \infty }{ \abs{ \frac{ a_{ n + 1 } }{ a_{ n } } } } \gt 1 </m>, and consider the positive real number
            
            <md>
              r = \frac{ L + 1 }{ 2 } = L - \frac{ L - 1 }{ 2 } \gt 1
            </md>.
            
            Then there is a natural number <m> m \in \N </m> so that <m> \abs{ \frac{ a_{ n + 1 } }{ a_{ n } } } \geq r </m> for all indices <m> n \geq m </m>. In particular, we see that
            
            <md>
              \abs{ a_{ n } } \geq \of{ \frac{ \abs{ a_{ m } } }{ r^{ m } } } r^{ n } \geq \frac{ \abs{ a_{ m } } }{ r^{ m } }
            </md>
            
            for all indices <m> n \geq m </m>, and so the sequence <m> \of{ a_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> does not converge to <m> 0 </m>. We conclude by <xref ref="proposition-convergent-series-have-convergent-terms"/> implies that the series
            
            <md>
              \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ a_{ n } }
            </md>
            
            diverges.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </proof>
  </proposition>

  <example xml:id="example-applying-the-ratio-test">
    <title>
      Applying the ratio test
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be the sequence of real numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \R </m> defined by the formula
        
        <md>
          x_{ n } = \frac{ 1 }{ n! }
        </md>.
        
        We observe that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \limsup_{ n \to \infty }{ \abs{ \frac{ x_{ n + 1 } }{ x_{ n } } } } \amp = \limsup_{ n \to \infty }{ \frac{ \frac{ 1 }{ \of{ n + 1 }! } }{ \frac{ 1 }{ n! } } } = \limsup_{ n \to \infty }{ \frac{ n! }{ \of{ n + 1 }! } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \limsup_{ n \to \infty }{ \frac{ 1 }{ n + 1 } } = 0 \lt 1
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        The <xref ref="proposition-ratio-test" text="title"/> now implies that the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> converges absolutely.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  
  <p>
    Since the <xref ref="proposition-ratio-test" text="title"/> is not always conclusive, we might try to seek out stronger convergence/divergence tests for series. The <term> root test </term> is one such result. 
  </p>
  
  <proposition xml:id="proposition-root-test">
    <title>
      Root test
    </title>
    
    <creator>
      Augustin-Louis Cauchy
    </creator>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of real numbers, and let
        
        <md>
          C = \limsup_{ n \to \infty }{ \sqrt[ n ]{ \abs{ x_{ n } } } }
        </md>.
        
        If <m> C \lt 1 </m>, then the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } }
        </md>.
        
        converges absolutely. On the other hand, if <m> C \gt 1 </m>, then this series diverges.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> r = \frac{ C + 1 }{ 2 } </m>. If <m> C \lt 1 </m>, then
        
        <md>
          C \lt r \lt 1
        </md>.
        
        Thus there is a natural number <m> m \in \N </m> so that <m> \sqrt[ n ]{ \abs{ x_{ n } } } \leq r </m> for all indices <m> n \geq m </m>. We observe that
        
        <md>
          0 \leq \abs{ x_{ n } } = \of{ \sqrt[ n ]{ \abs{ x_{ n } } } }^{ n } \leq r^{ n }
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq m </m>. The <xref ref="theorem-direct-comparison-test" text="title"/> now implies that the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ \abs{ x_{ n } } }
        </md>
        
        converges, since we compare with the geometric series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ r^{ n } } </m>. <xref ref="corollary-absolute-convergence-implies-convergence"/> now implies that the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } }
        </md>
        
        converges absolutely.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        On the other hand, if <m> C \gt 1 </m>, then
        
        <md>
          1 \lt r \lt C
        </md>.
        
        Thus there are infinitely many indices <m> n \in \N </m> so that <m> \sqrt[ n ]{ \abs{ x_{ n } } } \geq r \gt 1 </m>. In particular, <m> \abs{ x_{ n } } \geq r \gt 1 </m> for infinitely many indices <m> n \in \N </m>, and so the sequence <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> does not converge to <m> 0 </m>. Thus <xref ref="proposition-convergent-series-have-convergent-terms"/> implies that the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } }
        </md>
        
        diverges.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  
  <p>
    If the <xref ref="proposition-root-test" text="title"/> makes a conclusion about the convergence/divergence of a given series, then so will the <xref ref="proposition-ratio-test" text="title"/>. However, the converse implication is not true; the series in the following example is easily addressed by the <xref ref="proposition-root-test" text="title"/>, but the <xref ref="proposition-ratio-test" text="title"/> would not be of any use.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="example-applying-the-root-test">
    <title>
      Applying the <xref ref="proposition-root-test" text="title"/>
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be the sequence of real numbers <m> x_{ n } \in \R </m> defined piecewise by the formula
        
        <md>
          x_{ n } =
            \begin{cases}
              \frac{ 1 }{ 2^{ n } } \amp n \textrm{ is even} \\
              \frac{ 1 }{ 3^{ n } } \amp n \textrm{ is odd}
            \end{cases}
        </md>.
        
        We observe that
        
        <md>
          \sqrt[ n ]{ \abs{ x_{ n } } } = \sqrt[ n ]{ x_{ n } } =
            \begin{cases}
              \frac{ 1 }{ 2 } \amp n \textrm{ is even} \\
              \frac{ 1 }{ 3 } \amp n \textrm{ is odd}
            \end{cases}
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \in \N </m>, and so
        
        <md>
          \limsup_{ n \to \infty }{ \sqrt[ n ]{ \abs{ x_{ n } } } } = \frac{ 1 }{ 2 } \lt 1
        </md>.
        
        The <xref ref="proposition-root-test" text="title"/> now implies that the series <m> \sum_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty }{ x_{ n } } </m> converges absolutely.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  
  <p>
    We can also compare infinite series with improper integrals. We present the following result without proof:
  </p>
  
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-integral-test">
    <title>
      Integral test
    </title>
    
    <creator>
      Colin Maclaurin; Augustin-Louis Cauchy
    </creator>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> f \colon \clopint{ a }{ \infty } \to \clopint{ 0 }{ \infty } </m> be a continuous and non-increasing non-negative real-valued function defined on the half-closed, half-open interval <m> \clopint{ a }{ \infty } </m>. Then the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = a }^{ \infty }{ f \of{ n } }
        </md>
        
        converges if and only if the improper integral
        
        <md>
          \int_{ a }^{ \infty }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </md>
        
        converges.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <example xml:id="example-p-series">
    <title>
      <m> p </m> series
    </title>
    
    <idx>
      <m> p </m>-series
    </idx>
    
    <idx>
      <m> p </m>-test
    </idx>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term> <m> p </m>-series </term> associated to a positive real number <m> 0 \lt p \lt \infty </m> is the series
        
        <md>
          \sum_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty }{ \frac{ 1 }{ n^{ p } } }
        </md>.
        
        Together with <xref ref="example-improper-riemann-integration"/>, the <xref ref="theorem-integral-test" text="title"/> implies that the <m> p </m>-series converges when <m> p \gt 1 </m> and diverges to <m> \infty </m> when <m> 0 \lt p \leq 1 </m>. This fact is sometimes called the <term> <m> p </m>-test </term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      The <m> p </m>-series introduced in <xref ref="example-p-series"/> are ubiquitous, and the values of such convergent series are related to the Riemann zeta function. Of particular importance is the <term> harmonic series </term>, which is the divergent <m> p </m>-series obtained in the case <m> p = 1 </m>. 
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</subsection>
